    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d163">

                <pb facs="0259" n="153" sameAs="frus:frus1933-39/pg153"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">800.51W89 U.S.S.R./137a: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Bullitt</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1934-10-01T12:00:00-05:00">October 1,
                            1934—noon</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>264. The President concurs with our view that it will not be necessary for you to
                    remain in Moscow until Troyanovsky’s
                    arrival. You and we have done all that is possible in the way of making a most
                    liberal proposal. Our information is to the effect that Troyanovsky believes it should be accepted and
                    that his trip to Moscow is on his own initiative to endeavor to persuade his
                    Government to accept it. <pb facs="0260" n="154" xml:id="pg154"/> This being
                    assumed, you may consider that it is best to let him talk the matter to a
                    conclusion without our further intervention. Accordingly, you can leave Moscow
                    whenever you may think proper to do so.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
